<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferrara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferrara is a city of Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, near the Po. From the 13th century it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a>, whose court made it a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> art, music and letters, home to the poets Ariosto and Tasso. When the main Este line died out in 1597 the Papal States took the city, and its planned Renaissance extension, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea">Addizione Erculea</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferrara.jpg" alt="Ferrara" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nbisi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castello_dall%27alto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…annexed Avignon and the Comtat Venaissin in 1791, Napoleon forced Pius VI to cede the Legations of Bologna, <b>Ferrara</b> and Romagna at the Treaty of Tolentino in 1797, and in 1798 French troops proclaimed a Roman Republic and carried the pope into exile, where he died in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…14th to the 16th century a spiritual revival grew from the preaching friars, lay movements such as the devotio moderna, printing and Christian humanism, and councils with reforming agendas met at Constance in 1415, at Basel, <b>Ferrara</b> and Florence, and at the Fifth Council of the Lateran (1512–1517).</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The council that met at <b>Ferrara</b> and then Florence in 1438–1439 to reunite the Greek and Latin churches brought Byzantine scholars to Italy: Gemistos Plethon lectured in Florence on Plato, and Bessarion, made a cardinal of the Roman Church in 1439, settled in Italy and later gave his…</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>With the mediation of the Pope, Venice, Milan, Florence and the Gonzaga signed a peace at <b>Ferrara</b>, ending the second campaign of the Wars in Lombardy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Eugene IV presided over the first solemn session of the council he had moved from Basel to <b>Ferrara</b>, attended by the Eastern Roman Emperor and the Patriarch of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The members of the council who had stayed at Basel after Eugene IV moved it to <b>Ferrara</b> affirmed its ecumenical character and suspended the pope.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
